<% content_for :title, "Tools — Tinkerex" %>

<div class="page-head"><h1>Tools</h1></div>

<%#
  BUILT IN, ABOVE THE ADD-ONS (2026-10-21; plan 2026-10-21-calculator-modal.md). The calculator is a
  core feature — a modal that opens over any page (layouts/_calculator.html.erb) — so its card is
  written here and is not an addon card in disguise: it is `.tool-card`, never `.addon-card`, so
  every `.addon-card` on this page is still one a manifest made — which is what
  test/controllers/tools/home_controller_test.rb counts. Its own grid and heading keep the two
  kinds apart; the inner head/icon/title/description classes are shared for their look.

  The card OPENS the modal (`data-calculator-open`) and offers the sidebar pin. At phone width,
  where the tab bar keeps its four destinations and there is no rail, this card IS the way to the
  calculator (the Tools tab). `id="calculator"` is where the pinned rail link's href lands when its
  click is not the controller's to handle (a modified click).
%>
<section class="tools-section" aria-labelledby="tools-built-in">
  <h2 class="tools-section-head" id="tools-built-in">Built in</h2>
  <div class="tools-grid">
    <div class="tool-card calculator-card" id="calculator">
      <span class="addon-card-head">
        <span class="addon-icon"><%= rail_icon(:calculator) %></span>
        <span class="addon-title">Calculator</span>
      </span>
      <span class="addon-desc muted">Lengths in mm, cm, m, inches and feet, inch fractions, and
        conversions. Save a result to a project. <span class="calculator-card-keys">Opens over any page
        with <kbd><%= calculator_shortcut %></kbd>.</span></span>
      <span class="calculator-card-actions">
        <button type="button" class="btn small calculator-card-open" data-calculator-open aria-haspopup="dialog"
                aria-keyshortcuts="<%= CalculationsHelper::CALCULATOR_KEYS %>">Open calculator</button>
        <%# A button_to per state, so the label says what the click DOES. Plain `.btn`: the accent
            is not spent on this page (design-system.md rule 13). %>
        <% if current_user.calculator_pinned? %>
          <%= button_to "Unpin from sidebar", tools_calculator_pin_path, method: :delete,
                        class: "btn small calculator-pin", form_class: "calculator-pin-form" %>
        <% else %>
          <%= button_to "Pin to sidebar", tools_calculator_pin_path, method: :post,
                        class: "btn small calculator-pin", form_class: "calculator-pin-form" %>
        <% end %>
      </span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<h2 class="tools-section-head" id="tools-add-ons">Add-ons</h2>

<%# WHY A TOOL NEVER APPEARED, for the author editing a bundle — development and test only
    (Tools::HomeController#index decides). Production makers never see it; the log keeps the clue. %>
<% if @addon_problems.any? %>
  <div class="form-errors flash-alert addon-problems" role="status">
    <%# "PROBLEMS", NOT "TOOLS THAT WEREN'T LOADED": a duplicate key is reported while both entries
        are still served (Registry.problems), so the count is of problems, not of missing tools. %>
    <p><%= pluralize(@addon_problems.size, "problem") %> in <code>manifests.json</code>:</p>
    <ul>
      <% @addon_problems.each do |problem| %>
        <li><code><%= problem[:key] %></code> — <%= problem[:reason] %></li>
      <% end %>
    </ul>
  </div>
<% end %>

<%# THE FIRST-RUN STATE. With no addons this page was a title and a thousand pixels of void — the
    worst first-run surface in the app (UI-cohesion spec §2.4), and reached by every fresh
    self-host, since a box with no addon source configured lands here with nothing. It says what a
    tool IS before it says there are none, and it distinguishes "none configured" from "configured
    but nothing came back", because those need different things done about them and the only
    existing signal was a `Rails.logger.warn` nobody reads.

    TWO READERS, SO TWO LAYERS (onboarding, 2026-10-13). The maker reads the paragraph and can do
    nothing about either state but ask, so it says what is true in their words and points at Help.
    The settings that fix it belong to the person running the server and sit behind the collapsed
    line, where help_page_test.rb asserts they still are: moved, not deleted. %>
<% if @addons.empty? %>
  <div class="empty">
    <p>Add-ons are small apps that run inside Tinkerex and save their output straight into
      your library. The box generator and the OpenSCAD editor are two.</p>
    <% if @addon_source_configured %>
      <p class="muted">No tools came back from this server just now. The person running it can
        check on them — <%= link_to "Help", help_path(anchor: "tools") %> says how.</p>
      <details class="muted">
        <summary>For the person running this server</summary>
        An addon source is configured, but it returned nothing. It may be unreachable, or it may
        be serving no bundles yet.
      </details>
    <% else %>
      <p class="muted">No tools are installed on this server. The person running it can add
        them — <%= link_to "Help", help_path(anchor: "tools") %> says how.</p>
      <details class="muted">
        <summary>For the person running this server</summary>
        Point <code>ADDON_SOURCE_URL</code> at a bundle server, or <code>ADDON_SOURCE_PATH</code>
        at a directory of bundles, and they appear here.
      </details>
    <% end %>
  </div>
<% end %>

<div class="tools-grid">
  <% @addons.each do |addon| %>
    <% if addon.available? %>
      <%= link_to tools_addon_path(addon.key), class: "addon-card" do %>
        <span class="addon-card-head">
          <span class="addon-icon"><%= addon.icon %></span>
          <span class="addon-title"><%= addon.title %></span>
        </span>
        <span class="addon-desc muted"><%= addon.description %></span>
        <% if addon.permissions.any? %>
          <span class="addon-permissions addon-card-foot muted">
            This tool can:
            <%= addon.permissions.map { |p| Maker::Addons::Permissions.label_for(p["name"]) }.to_sentence %>
          </span>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    <% else %>
      <div class="addon-card disabled" title="<%= addon.unavailable_reason %>">
        <span class="addon-card-head">
          <span class="addon-icon"><%= addon.icon %></span>
          <span class="addon-title"><%= addon.title %></span>
        </span>
        <span class="addon-desc muted"><%= addon.unavailable_reason %></span>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
  <%# NO HARDCODED CARDS. The last one was openscad's, kept while it still had its own controller;
      it migrated to a bundle and the block went with it. Every card above comes from a manifest,
      which is what makes publishing a bundle the whole deploy. A card that has to be written here
      is the signal that an addon has not actually migrated. %>
</div>
